1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,633
In this town, a giant die rolls every sunrise. Today, it landed on three.

2
00:00:05,633 --> 00:00:11,233
All across the plaza, people rose off the ground. A baker grabbed his stall as his shoes floated away.

3
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:12,733
Everyone was rising. Except Leo.

4
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:21,033
He'd tied himself to the clock tower. Leo hated surprises. He even kept a notebook listing what to do if the town suddenly lost gravity.

5
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:26,433
Every roll changed the law. One floated loose objects. Two made everyone walk backward. Three lifted people into the air.

6
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:29,533
But in thirty years, the die had never landed on six.

7
00:00:29,533 --> 00:00:36,633
Leo had counted the rolls. And he'd noticed something strange: whenever six almost appeared, the die made the exact same wobble.

8
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:40,667
So while the town floated above him, Leo slipped underneath the stone platform.

9
00:00:40,667 --> 00:00:48,067
There was a brass paddle and a crank. Somebody had been knocking the die away from six before it could settle.

10
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:49,833
Mayor Pip was holding the handle.

11
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:57,000
Pip looked ten, but he'd started the game thirty years earlier. Nobody inside the town grew older. Outside, his family had.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,933
Pip held their faded photograph.

13
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:03,500
"If six lands, the gate opens," he whispered. "What if they've forgotten me?"

14
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,567
Leo had an answer for almost everything. But not that.

15
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:10,967
He pulled a small pin from the machine, stopping the paddle, and placed it in Pip's hand.

16
00:01:10,967 --> 00:01:14,133
"You can put it back," Leo said. "I won't stop you."

17
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:17,967
The next morning, the die didn't land on six. Neither did the next.

18
00:01:17,967 --> 00:01:26,167
Five mornings went by. Pip watched each roll, holding the pin. Once, he almost returned it. Then he looked at the photograph and put it down.

19
00:01:26,167 --> 00:01:28,967
On the sixth sunrise, the die began to turn.

20
00:01:28,967 --> 00:01:30,533
It rocked toward six.

21
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:32,900
Pip reached for the pin, and stopped himself.

22
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:35,000
The die settled.

23
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,600
Six.

24
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:43,000
The town gate swung open. Pip ran through it, past streets he barely recognized, until he reached his old house.

25
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,667
An older woman stood in the doorway. She took one look at him and said his name.

26
00:01:47,667 --> 00:01:51,467
Inside was a small chair by the kitchen table. Everything had changed except that chair.

27
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:52,967
"We kept your chair," she said.

28
00:01:52,967 --> 00:01:57,100
And Leo, watching from the open gate, finally closed his notebook without planning tomorrow.

